1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,003
Aarav opens a dead phone charger.

2
00:00:02,353 --> 00:00:06,149
He finds tiny black parts,
each with a stripe at one end.

3
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:11,098
Inside each one is just p-type
silicon joined to n-type silicon.

4
00:00:11,598 --> 00:00:14,654
Yet together they act like
a one-way gate for current.

5
00:00:15,004 --> 00:00:16,810
Where does that gate come from?

6
00:00:16,810 --> 00:00:18,701
It builds itself, at the joint.

7
00:00:19,351 --> 00:00:21,978
Picture two rooms joined by an open door.

8
00:00:22,328 --> 00:00:24,412
One room is packed with people.

9
00:00:24,412 --> 00:00:26,197
The other is nearly empty.

10
00:00:26,547 --> 00:00:29,540
People drift through the door
to where it is less crowded.

11
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:33,321
Spreading from crowded to
empty is called diffusion.

12
00:00:33,321 --> 00:00:35,136
Smells spread the same way.

13
00:00:35,786 --> 00:00:37,286
Now the real thing.

14
00:00:37,286 --> 00:00:39,900
One silicon crystal, doped two ways.

15
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:43,085
The left side is p-type, full of holes.

16
00:00:43,685 --> 00:00:46,487
The right side is n-type,
full of free electrons.

17
00:00:47,087 --> 00:00:49,953
The boundary between
them is the p-n junction.

18
00:00:50,603 --> 00:00:52,760
Free electrons diffuse from n into p.

19
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:56,619
Holes diffuse the other
way, from p into n.

20
00:00:57,219 --> 00:01:00,483
Near the junction they meet,
and electrons fill holes.

21
00:01:00,483 --> 00:01:01,501
Both disappear.

22
00:01:02,101 --> 00:01:05,041
But each electron left
behind a donor atom.

23
00:01:05,391 --> 00:01:08,224
That donor is now a fixed positive ion.

24
00:01:08,224 --> 00:01:09,471
It cannot move.

25
00:01:10,071 --> 00:01:14,337
Each filled hole leaves a fixed
negative acceptor ion on the p side.

26
00:01:14,937 --> 00:01:17,658
So a thin layer forms with
ions but no free carriers.

27
00:01:18,258 --> 00:01:20,691
It has been emptied, or
depleted, of carriers.

28
00:01:20,691 --> 00:01:22,160
This is the depletion layer.

29
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:26,483
It is very thin, often less than
a thousandth of a millimetre.

30
00:01:27,132 --> 00:01:28,863
Now the ions start pushing back.

31
00:01:29,213 --> 00:01:32,523
Plus ions on the n side,
minus ions on the p side.

32
00:01:33,123 --> 00:01:37,057
Together they make an electric
field, pointing from n to p.

33
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:40,316
This field pushes stray
electrons back to the n side.

34
00:01:40,916 --> 00:01:43,340
Carriers moved by a field
make a drift current.

35
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:46,287
Drift flows opposite to diffusion.

36
00:01:46,887 --> 00:01:49,822
At first diffusion wins,
and the layer grows.

37
00:01:50,172 --> 00:01:53,053
The wider it grows, the
stronger the push back.

38
00:01:53,703 --> 00:01:55,678
Soon drift equals diffusion.

39
00:01:55,678 --> 00:01:58,468
They cancel, and no net current flows.

40
00:01:59,118 --> 00:02:02,483
The junction builds its
own wall, and then stops.

41
00:02:03,283 --> 00:02:05,470
Let's watch it settle on the breadboard.

42
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:09,694
We join the two sides and connect
a current meter across them.

43
00:02:10,044 --> 00:02:11,830
The scope shows two currents.

44
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:13,017
Grey is diffusion.

45
00:02:13,017 --> 00:02:14,070
Amber is drift.

46
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:17,934
At the instant of joining,
diffusion is large.

47
00:02:18,284 --> 00:02:22,512
The layer widens, the field
grows, and drift rises to meet it.

48
00:02:23,162 --> 00:02:24,691
Now they are equal.

49
00:02:24,691 --> 00:02:26,326
The meter reads zero.

50
00:02:26,326 --> 00:02:27,292
Equilibrium.

51
00:02:27,942 --> 00:02:31,025
The ions leave a potential
difference across the layer.

52
00:02:31,375 --> 00:02:34,357
It is called the barrier
potential, V naught.

53
00:02:35,007 --> 00:02:37,776
In silicon it is about point seven volts.

54
00:02:38,426 --> 00:02:42,974
An electron must climb this energy
hill to cross, like a ball rolling up

55
00:02:42,974 --> 00:02:43,874
a slope.

56
00:02:44,387 --> 00:02:45,378
Worked example.

57
00:02:45,378 --> 00:02:46,971
How strong is that field?

58
00:02:47,321 --> 00:02:49,541
Take a barrier of point seven volts.

59
00:02:50,141 --> 00:02:53,201
And a depletion layer
half a micrometre wide.

60
00:02:53,801 --> 00:02:58,158
For a nearly uniform field, E
equals V naught divided by d.

61
00:02:58,858 --> 00:03:03,017
Point seven divided by point
five times ten to the minus six.

62
00:03:03,717 --> 00:03:07,545
E is one point four times ten
to the six volts per metre.

63
00:03:08,245 --> 00:03:12,564
To cross, an electron needs at
least point seven electron volts.

64
00:03:13,264 --> 00:03:17,492
That is one point one two times
ten to the minus nineteen joules.

65
00:03:18,142 --> 00:03:19,603
Now, the exam trap.

66
00:03:19,953 --> 00:03:22,609
Can a voltmeter read
the barrier potential?

67
00:03:22,609 --> 00:03:24,106
Many students say yes.

68
00:03:24,456 --> 00:03:25,610
It reads zero.

69
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:29,173
Contact voltages at the metal
leads cancel it exactly.

70
00:03:29,823 --> 00:03:31,008
Where is this asked?

71
00:03:31,008 --> 00:03:33,831
PUC, CBSE and ISC boards
ask how the junction forms.

72
00:03:34,181 --> 00:03:38,825
JEE Main asks for the field in the
depletion layer, as in July twenty twenty

73
00:03:38,825 --> 00:03:39,725
two.

74
00:03:39,963 --> 00:03:40,863
Try this.

75
00:03:40,662 --> 00:03:43,499
A junction's depletion layer
is two micrometres wide.

76
00:03:43,499 --> 00:03:47,326
Its field is three point five times
ten to the five volts per metre.

77
00:03:47,326 --> 00:03:50,964
Find the barrier, and the least
energy for an electron to cross.

78
00:03:56,364 --> 00:03:57,264
A.

79
00:03:56,762 --> 00:04:01,218
Point seven volts, and one point one
two times ten to the minus nineteen

80
00:04:01,218 --> 00:04:02,118
joules.

81
00:04:02,209 --> 00:04:04,619
Barrier V naught equals E times d.

82
00:04:04,969 --> 00:04:09,517
Three point five times ten to the
five, times two times ten to the minus

83
00:04:09,517 --> 00:04:10,417
six.

84
00:04:10,016 --> 00:04:11,825
That is point seven volts.

85
00:04:12,175 --> 00:04:14,482
The energy is e times V naught.

86
00:04:14,482 --> 00:04:19,367
That is point seven electron volts,
or one point one two times ten to the

87
00:04:19,367 --> 00:04:20,957
minus nineteen joules.

88
00:04:21,607 --> 00:04:23,154
Screenshot this card.

89
00:04:26,354 --> 00:04:28,157
Next, diode V-I characteristics.

90
00:04:28,157 --> 00:04:30,803
What happens when a
battery pushes on the wall?
